    <span>·</span>
    <time datetime="2026-10-15">October 2026</time>
    <span>·</span>
    <span>Systems & Visualization Case Study</span>
  </div>

  <!-- Direct Answer Executive Summary -->
  <div class="direct-answer">
    <p><strong>Executive Summary:</strong> Flight Sun Tracker is an aviation solar positioning tool calculating left and right cabin sunlight along great-circle routes. Enter departure time and duration to find seat shade or scenic golden hour views before boarding.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Hard Quantitative Statistics -->
  <div class="metrics-strip">
    <div class="metric-pill">
      <span class="metric-label">Language</span>
      <span class="metric-value">TypeScript</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-pill">
      <span class="metric-label">Renderer</span>
      <span class="metric-value">Selectable globe views</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-pill">
      <span class="metric-label">Calculations</span>
      <span class="metric-value">SunCalc</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-pill">
      <span class="metric-label">Route sampling</span>
      <span class="metric-value">5 minutes</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>How to check sunlight on a flight</h2>
    <p>To check sunlight on a commercial flight, enter the departure and arrival airport IATA codes, flight date, and departure time. The flight sun tracker calculates the great-circle geodesic trajectory and solar azimuth angles to determine whether the left (port) or right (starboard) side receives direct sunlight, glare, or golden hour views throughout the journey.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the live app and select <strong>Configure route</strong>.</li>
      <li>Select the departure and arrival airports, departure date and time, and estimated air duration.</li>
      <li>Compute the route, then choose <strong>Avoid sun glare</strong> or <strong>Scenic sun &amp; golden hour</strong>.</li>
      <li>Compare the recommended side, solar events and timeline. Confirm the time zone used for the departure input before comparing against a ticket.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure>
      <img src="/projects/flight-sun-tracker/app-preview.png" alt="Flight Sun Tracker showing JFK to London, the seat-side verdict, route globe and cabin sunlight display" width="1280" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px" />
      <figcaption>Live interface captured on 24 October 2026. This preview uses the app's displayed route; the fixed API example below uses a separate, explicit UTC departure.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Worked example: JFK to London Heathrow</h2>
    <p>A request to the live recommendation endpoint was checked on 24 October 2026 using these inputs:</p>
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Route</th><td>JFK to LHR</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Departure</th><td>25 October 2026, 10:00 UTC</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Duration</th><td>420 minutes, or 7 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Preference</th><td>Shade</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Returned recommendation</th><td>Left side</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The response placed sunrise near 11:00 UTC and sunset near 16:50 UTC, with the sun on the right at both sampled events. These are model results for this route and time, not measured cabin observations. Changing the date, time or duration can change the recommendation.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Calculation method and limits</h2>
    <p>The <a href="https://github.com/smresponsibilities/flight-sun-tracker/blob/main/src/lib/flightCalculator.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TypeScript calculator</a> interpolates a great-circle route at five-minute intervals, gets solar positions from SunCalc and compares solar azimuth with the aircraft's heading. The Next.js application offers multiple globe views; it is not a zero-dependency canvas-only application.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A great-circle path is an approximation. Actual routing, delays, altitude and aircraft turns can differ.</li>
      <li>Weather, window geometry, wing obstruction and cabin shades are not guarantees provided by the model.</li>
      <li>Five-minute sampling limits event-time precision. Confidence labels are app heuristics, not independently calibrated probabilities.</li>
      <li>There is no universal sunny side for eastbound or westbound travel. Route, latitude, season and departure time matter.</li>
      <li>The live endpoint accepts a limited date range. The dated example records a verified result; it is not a permanently valid booking input.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flight-faq" aria-labelledby="flight-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="flight-faq-heading">Flight Sunlight Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which side of an airplane gets more sun on eastbound flights?</h3>
      <p>On typical daytime eastbound flights in the Northern Hemisphere (such as New York to London), the right (starboard) side faces south toward the sun and receives direct sunlight and glare, while the left (port) side stays shaded.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How does Flight Sun Tracker calculate aircraft solar position?</h3>
      <p>The TypeScript engine interpolates great-circle geodesic waypoints at five-minute intervals, computes local timestamps, and evaluates solar azimuth and elevation via SunCalc to determine the relative angle between the sun and the fuselage.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Does Flight Sun Tracker work without internet during a flight?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Flight Sun Tracker runs client-side calculations in the browser. Once the route is computed, solar trajectory calculations and 3D globe visualizations operate offline without requiring continuous in-flight Wi-Fi.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Source Code &amp; Verification</h2>
    <p>The code is fully open-source and maintained on GitHub. Explore the repository and implementation details:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Live Interactive Application:</strong> <a href="https://flight-sun-tracker-shivam-mahajans-projects.vercel.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">flight-sun-tracker-shivam-mahajans-projects.vercel.app ↗</a></li>
